From 65cc9789c13c0a207721551e0ebbc630bcd90b91 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 14:51:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add Zombie Survival to Games & Worlds, next to
 CollinSurvivalCraft

The owner asked for their zombie survival game to sit in the left menu's
Games & Worlds group, beside CollinSurvivalCraft. It is a separate project
(eru/zombie-survival-game), so it ships the way the PC does: its finished
build lives in public/zombie-survival/ and /zombie-survival frames it under
a slim header. It has no Supabase or account code; runs save in this
browser's IndexedDB.

Every way an embed like this breaks is silent, so the page and its test
cover each one. A deploy without the game's files does not 404 - the host
answers with Jackie's own index.html, which would boot the app inside its
own frame - so the page checks the frame's title after load and says the
game is missing instead. The build is made with relative paths, since an
absolute /assets/ would load Jackie's bundles. The service worker leaves
the game out of the precache (every visitor would download it whether
they play or not) and out of the SPA navigation fallback (which answers
the frame with Jackie's shell; checked in a browser with the worker
controlling the page).

Leaving the page destroys the frame, and a save the game starts while it
is torn down may never commit, so Back and Restart ask the game to save
first over postMessage and wait for its answer. The game keeps its own
autosave rules (not while something is hunting you, not mid-climb); when
it refuses, or does not answer within 2.5 s, the page says why and asks
before the run is lost, rather than leaving as if it had saved. Open in
a tab has this copy save and step aside, so two copies never overwrite
one autosave.

Full screen covers only the game, which is also the way to play without
the floating nav bar over its HUD, so that button carries a label. On a
device with no fine pointer the page says plainly there are no touch
controls yet. The route is in the manifest, so the guide, SANDi and the
nav bar editor find it; it gets a curated nav-bar entry with its own icon.

